## The Bug: Loop Variable Captured by Reference

In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is **a single variable reused across all iterations**. Your goroutines don't capture the *value* of `i` at launch time — they capture a *reference* to the same variable.

By the time any goroutine actually executes `fmt.Println(i)`, the loop has already finished, and `i` holds its final value: **5**.

### The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine function. This creates a new local variable for each call that holds the value at the time of the call:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

### Why this works

Each call to `go func(i int){...}(i)` passes the *current* value of the loop variable as an argument. The parameter `i` in the function signature is a distinct local variable per goroutine, so each one prints the value it was given.

> **Note:** Starting with Go 1.22, the loop variable semantics changed so that each iteration gets its own `i`, and your original code would work as expected. But since you're on Go 1.21, the parameter-passing fix is necessary.

